matrix(String) -- make a matrix using classic Macaulay syntax
*************************************************************

  * Function: *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)matrix,
  * Usage: 
        matrix s
  * Inputs:
      * s, a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, in the form:
        "f1,f2,...,fr;g1,...,gr;...;h1,...,hr"
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: (Macaulay2Doc)matrix_lpList_rp, => ..., default value
        null,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,

Description
===========

Creates a matrix using an abbreviated format. Each polynomial has the form
described in *note Classic: Top,.  The rows of the matrix are separated by
semicolons, and within each row, the polynomials are separated by commas.  Any
entry that is missing is assumed to be 0. Spaces and newline characters are
ignored.
+---------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/32003[a..d,x_1..x_4];            |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i2 : N = matrix "a,b,c,d;x[1],x[2],x[3],x[4]"|
|                                             |
|o2 = | a   b   c   d   |                     |
|     | x_1 x_2 x_3 x_4 |                     |
|                                             |
|             2      4                        |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R                         |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i3 : M = matrix "ad-2c3,(a-b)2+1,ab;,abc-1," |
|                                             |
|o3 = | -2c3+ad a2-2ab+b2+1 ab |              |
|     | 0       abc-1       0  |              |
|                                             |
|             2      3                        |
|o3 : Matrix R  <-- R                         |
+---------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note poly(String): poly_lpString_rp, -- make a polynomial using classic
    Macaulay syntax
  * ideal(String) (missing documentation)

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note matrix(String): matrix_lpString_rp, -- make a matrix using classic
    Macaulay syntax
